roomlerd for Debian/Ubuntu
==========================

The daemon binary is /usr/bin/roomlerd.

Which unit do I want?
---------------------

The package ships TWO units. Pick ONE — running both enrolls the same host
twice and they fight over the same identity.

  roomler.service   (systemd --user, runs as YOU)   -> desktop host
  roomlerd.service  (systemd system, runs as root)  -> headless / server / WSL

                            | roomler.service | roomlerd.service
  --------------------------+-----------------+------------------
  screen capture + input    | yes             | opt-in (drm_capture + uinput)
  overlay mesh (roomler0)   | no              | yes (TUN needs CAP_NET_ADMIN)
  unattended self-update    | no              | yes (installing a .deb needs root)
  starts before login       | no              | yes

Want remote desktop? Use the user unit. Want a mesh node that keeps itself
up to date with nobody logged in? Use the system unit.

Desktop host (systemd --user)
-----------------------------

  # 1. Generate an enrollment JWT at https://roomler.ai/ (Admin -> Agents).
  # 2. Enroll as your normal user, NOT root:
  roomlerd enroll --server https://roomler.ai \
                  --token <paste-enrollment-jwt> \
                  --name "$(hostname)"

  # 3. Optional smoke test — needs an active graphical session (X11, or
  #    Wayland since 0.4.130); a tty login won't capture anything.
  RUST_LOG=roomlerd=debug,webrtc=info roomlerd run

  systemctl --user daemon-reload
  systemctl --user enable --now roomler.service
  journalctl --user -u roomler.service -f

  # A headless host additionally needs lingering, or the user manager
  # (and the agent with it) exits when you log out:
  sudo loginctl enable-linger "$USER"

Self-update note: an unprivileged daemon cannot install a .deb. It logs an
error, raises ~/.config/roomler/needs-attention.txt, and KEEPS RUNNING —
upgrade by hand with `sudo dpkg -i`, or move to the system unit.

Headless / server / WSL (systemd system)
----------------------------------------

Config lives at a machine-global path, not in a home directory, because the
unit starts at boot before any login. Enroll against the SAME path you run
with — `enroll` creates the directory and writes the file 0600 (it holds a
bearer token):

  sudo roomlerd --config /etc/roomler/config.toml \
       enroll --server https://roomler.ai \
              --token <paste-enrollment-jwt> \
              --name "$(hostname)"

  sudo systemctl daemon-reload
  sudo systemctl enable --now roomlerd.service
  sudo journalctl -u roomlerd.service -f

For a different config path, drop ROOMLERD_CONFIG=/path/to/config.toml into
/etc/default/roomlerd.

Both flows above are what `install.sh --role daemon [--system]` automates.

Enabling the overlay mesh
-------------------------

Set `overlay_enabled = true` at the TOP LEVEL of config.toml — it is a
root-level key, so do not append it at the end of the file, where it would
land inside a [table] — then restart the daemon. The node generates and
persists its WireGuard key on the first overlay-enabled start.

The OS TUN needs CAP_NET_ADMIN, so this works under roomlerd.service (root)
and not under roomler.service. On a host that cannot run as root, set
ROOMLERD_OVERLAY_NETSTACK_SOCKS=<port> for the userspace netstack
instead: outbound mesh access through a loopback SOCKS5 proxy, no TUN and no
routes — but other nodes cannot reach services on this host.

Wayland
-------

Under the user unit, a Wayland session captures itself:

  GNOME   mutter's own screencast, from 0.4.130 (input from 0.4.132). It
          asks nobody. While the screen is locked GNOME shares nothing, and
          the viewer says to unlock it at the computer.
  KDE     the desktop portal, from 0.4.132 (input from 0.4.133). It asks
  others  the person at the screen once (KDE's "Remote Control" dialog),
          then remembers the answer.

The system unit captures a desktop, Wayland or X11, only once both of these
keys are set. They read the screen below the desktop and inject input into
whatever has focus, the login screen included, so nothing sets them for you:

  sudo roomler config set drm_capture true
  sudo roomler config set uinput true
  sudo systemctl restart roomlerd.service

Until then the viewer says "capturing it is switched off on this device" and
names these two commands. It never shows a black screen or calls a desktop
host headless.

What each desktop, install and session state gives, and every message the
viewer can show: docs/wayland.md in the roomler-ai repository.

